Yes. That’s the right direction: adaptation under conditions that reveal capability without requiring harm, deprivation, humiliation, or coercion.

You can design environments that make latent strategies surface through play, challenge, novelty, competition, cooperation, ambiguity, constrained resources, role-switching, puzzles, simulation, storytelling, games, scavenger hunts, creative building, or deliberately weird interfaces. The environment applies enough pressure to provoke adaptation, but the participant still experiences agency, curiosity, and reward.

The pattern is something like:

latent capability → safe pressure → adaptation → expression → observation → reflection → meta-adaptation

And then one level higher:

design the environment so that the way someone adapts teaches you how to design better environments for adaptation.

That is the meta-adaptive loop.

The humane constraint matters because stress can reveal capability, but it can also distort it. If the environment is punitive, you may mostly learn how someone behaves under threat. If it is playful and voluntary, you can often expose exploration, creativity, cooperation, improvisation, and identity with much less contamination.

So instead of “break the system and see what survives,” the better experimental primitive is:

“Give the system an interesting world and see what it invents.”

That works for humans, teams, AI agents, institutions, and even creative worlds.

And it is a much better way to discover what something can become than merely measuring what it already does.